  [1mApproaches[0m

  First, what [3mdoesn't[0m vary. In all three, the notification subsystem is the same plain-JS core, and it's the part worth
  designing:

  - [1m[38;5;153mevents.js[0m — an event source interface ([38;5;153msubscribe(handler)[39m), so the due-date scheduler is one implementation and a
    future server stream is another.
  - [1m[38;5;153mscheduler.js[0m — owns "what is due." A single tick compares [38;5;153mnow[39m against task [38;5;153mdueAt[39ms and emits [38;5;153mtask.due[39m / [38;5;153mtask.overdue[39m
    exactly once per task per transition.
  - [1m[38;5;153mnotifications.js[0m — the store: append, unread count, mark-read, dismiss, persist.
  - [1m[38;5;153mchannels.js[0m — a delivery interface. [38;5;153mcentre[39m and [38;5;153mtoast[39m are two implementations; a future OS-notification channel is a
    third.

  Those four have no DOM in them and no framework in them. The approaches differ only in the shell around them.

